WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:03.544
Welcome. In this first part, we will look at

2
00:00:03.567 --> 00:00:07.322
something you already know: how you usually approach a

3
00:00:07.344 --> 00:00:11.544
conversation, a decision, or a piece of work. The

4
00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:14.300
habits you rely on have helped you get things

5
00:00:14.322 --> 00:00:17.989
done. They can also shape what you notice and

6
00:00:18.011 --> 00:00:19.922
what you expect from other people.

7
00:00:20.916 --> 00:00:24.960
This learning is voluntary. You can pause, return to

8
00:00:24.982 --> 00:00:28.516
a section, follow the example with us, or think

9
00:00:28.538 --> 00:00:31.471
about a process you know. If you choose to

10
00:00:31.493 --> 00:00:35.093
make notes, keep names, case details, and other private

11
00:00:35.116 --> 00:00:38.027
information out of them. You do not have to

12
00:00:38.049 --> 00:00:40.338
share a personal story to take part.

13
00:00:42.283 --> 00:00:45.950
Across these lessons we will follow one work example.

14
00:00:46.639 --> 00:00:49.350
A division team is revising how people hear about

15
00:00:49.372 --> 00:00:52.617
and respond to a planning opportunity. There is a

16
00:00:52.639 --> 00:00:55.750
purpose and a decision deadline. The team has not

17
00:00:55.772 --> 00:00:58.661
yet settled on the best way to communicate, invite

18
00:00:58.683 --> 00:01:01.328
input, or explain what happened afterward.

19
00:01:02.071 --> 00:01:05.227
You can imagine yourself helping with a notice, a

20
00:01:05.249 --> 00:01:08.871
meeting, a recommendation, or another part of that work.

21
00:01:10.104 --> 00:01:13.638
Imagine that the team usually shares a long draft

22
00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.660
the morning of a meeting and asks for reactions

23
00:01:16.682 --> 00:01:20.127
before the meeting ends. Some people find the discussion

24
00:01:20.149 --> 00:01:23.482
useful. Others may need a different way to review

25
00:01:23.504 --> 00:01:26.704
or respond. At this point, we do not know

26
00:01:26.727 --> 00:01:28.749
who prefers what or why.

27
00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.698
You might be comfortable thinking out loud. You might

28
00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.276
prefer to read first, ask questions afterward, or have

29
00:01:36.298 --> 00:01:39.831
time to consult someone. A preference can change with

30
00:01:39.853 --> 00:01:43.431
the task, the amount of information, the people involved,

31
00:01:43.631 --> 00:01:46.298
and the time available. It is not a permanent

32
00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.364
label for you or anyone else.

33
00:01:48.902 --> 00:01:52.102
The useful question is not whether one style is

34
00:01:52.124 --> 00:01:55.213
right. Ask what the work needs, what the current

35
00:01:55.236 --> 00:01:58.480
routine assumes, and which arrangements would give people a

36
00:01:58.502 --> 00:02:02.524
usable opportunity to contribute. A format can be familiar

37
00:02:02.547 --> 00:02:05.702
to the team without being required for the decision.

38
00:02:07.003 --> 00:02:10.559
Suppose a participant says they would like more time

39
00:02:10.581 --> 00:02:14.002
before commenting. What do you know? You know they

40
00:02:14.024 --> 00:02:16.803
asked for more time. You may also know the

41
00:02:16.826 --> 00:02:19.914
decision date. You do not yet know why the

42
00:02:19.937 --> 00:02:23.380
person asked, what they might contribute, or whether an

43
00:02:23.402 --> 00:02:24.803
adjustment is possible.

44
00:02:25.677 --> 00:02:29.143
An assumption might sound like, “They are not interested,”

45
00:02:29.166 --> 00:02:33.499
or “They are not prepared.” Another assumption could be,

46
00:02:33.854 --> 00:02:37.121
“Everyone who prefers written comments thinks the same way.”

47
00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:40.721
Each moves beyond the information you have. A more

48
00:02:40.743 --> 00:02:43.321
useful next step is to ask what would help

49
00:02:43.343 --> 00:02:45.121
and check what the team can offer.

50
00:02:46.318 --> 00:02:49.229
This is also a chance to notice your own

51
00:02:49.251 --> 00:02:53.073
reaction. Perhaps you feel pressure because the deadline is

52
00:02:53.096 --> 00:02:56.873
close. That feeling is worth noticing, and it does

53
00:02:56.896 --> 00:02:59.896
not have to settle the question. You can acknowledge

54
00:02:59.918 --> 00:03:02.807
the constraint and still look for an option that

55
00:03:02.829 --> 00:03:04.784
respects the person's participation.

56
00:03:06.139 --> 00:03:09.206
The team may have some choices and not others.

57
00:03:09.672 --> 00:03:13.317
A decision date, a review step, an accessibility requirement,

58
00:03:13.539 --> 00:03:17.450
or a policy may need confirmation. Within those limits,

59
00:03:17.606 --> 00:03:20.272
staff may have discretion over how clearly they invite

60
00:03:20.294 --> 00:03:22.917
comments or how people can ask for support. Check

61
00:03:22.939 --> 00:03:25.206
rather than assume which is which.

62
00:03:26.041 --> 00:03:29.486
Roles matter too. A person who drafts an invitation

63
00:03:29.508 --> 00:03:32.486
may be able to suggest a change but not

64
00:03:32.508 --> 00:03:36.730
authorize a new deadline. A supervisor or another responsible

65
00:03:36.752 --> 00:03:40.286
office may need to decide. The person affected is

66
00:03:40.308 --> 00:03:42.730
the best source for what would make their participation

67
00:03:42.752 --> 00:03:46.752
workable; their preference still needs an honest response about

68
00:03:46.774 --> 00:03:49.063
what is possible and what must be checked.

69
00:03:49.621 --> 00:03:52.910
The goal is not to judge a colleague's intent.

70
00:03:53.554 --> 00:03:56.732
A team can mean to welcome everyone and still

71
00:03:56.754 --> 00:03:59.577
have a routine that is easier for some people

72
00:03:59.599 --> 00:04:03.354
to use than others. Looking at the effect gives

73
00:04:03.377 --> 00:04:05.399
you a practical place to begin.

74
00:04:06.436 --> 00:04:10.747
Return to the planning invitation. A participant asks for

75
00:04:10.769 --> 00:04:13.947
more time to review the draft. The decision date

76
00:04:13.969 --> 00:04:16.880
is approaching, and you have not yet confirmed whether

77
00:04:16.902 --> 00:04:19.924
the comment period can change. What would you do

78
00:04:19.947 --> 00:04:20.347
first?

79
00:04:21.089 --> 00:04:23.644
Pause here if you would like to consider your

80
00:04:23.667 --> 00:04:27.000
response. Choose an answer in the activity, then compare

81
00:04:27.022 --> 00:04:30.222
the reasoning. You can revisit the choices and change

82
00:04:30.244 --> 00:04:34.333
your answer. The activity is practice; the actual requirements

83
00:04:34.356 --> 00:04:37.800
and decision authority belong to the responsible people and

84
00:04:37.822 --> 00:04:38.311
offices.

85
00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:43.801
Choose one small routine in your own work, or

86
00:04:43.823 --> 00:04:47.468
stay with the planning example. Name the step, what

87
00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:51.023
it is meant to accomplish, and one expectation it

88
00:04:51.046 --> 00:04:54.179
makes about the person using it. Then write a

89
00:04:54.201 --> 00:04:56.801
question that could help you check that expectation.

90
00:04:57.731 --> 00:05:01.353
For example: “We usually ask for comments in a

91
00:05:01.376 --> 00:05:04.731
live meeting. What other response routes could fit the

92
00:05:04.753 --> 00:05:08.264
decision schedule, and whom should we ask before choosing?”

93
00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:11.042
You do not need to name a person or

94
00:05:11.064 --> 00:05:14.442
describe a private situation. The point is to make

95
00:05:14.464 --> 00:05:16.353
the next conversation more useful.

96
00:05:17.132 --> 00:05:19.710
In the next part, we will look at the

97
00:05:19.732 --> 00:05:24.443
Minnesota community briefs as one source of context. We

98
00:05:24.466 --> 00:05:27.510
will use them to prepare better questions while keeping

99
00:05:27.532 --> 00:05:30.843
the answer with the individual and the people affected

100
00:05:30.866 --> 00:05:31.710
by the work.
